# Line endings belong to the repository, not to the machine.
#
# odinfmt.json pins newline_style to CRLF and `just fmt-check` compares BYTES,
# so what puts CRLF in a working tree decides whether this repository builds at
# all. Until this line, nothing here did: the blobs are stored LF, and the only
# thing converting them on the way out was Git for Windows' SYSTEM gitconfig
# setting core.autocrlf=true -- an uncommitted, machine-global setting that
# happened to be on. `git -c core.autocrlf=false clone` produced a checkout
# where all thirteen .odin files failed the formatting check and `just build`
# refused to build anything; running the remedy that failure prints would then
# have rewritten every one of them and flipped the object store for everybody
# else.
#
# `text` stores LF and checks out CRLF, which is what the blobs already hold and
# what the working tree already looks like here -- so this states the state
# rather than changing it. Checked per FILE against what git itself reports for
# each path: a rule written for the wrong glob reads perfectly well and matches
# nothing.
*.odin text eol=crlf

# A fixture is EVIDENCE, not source.
#
# src\transcript\fixtures\ holds one real whisper.cpp run committed exactly as
# the Engine wrote it, systeminfo block and absolute model path and all, because
# the whole point of it is to pin the schema this design bets on (ADR-0001) --
# and, beside it, the two Markdown Transcripts that run must render as, one per
# Merge Profile. Those are compared BYTE FOR BYTE, front matter included, so a
# checkout that rewrote their endings would fail every comparison at once.
# Line-ending normalisation is an EDIT to that evidence. With core.autocrlf on
# and nothing said here, the 2335 bytes the Engine wrote were stored as 2226:
# the blob was not the file anyone was told it was, and the byte count the test
# documents was true only on a machine that happened to convert it back.
#
# It matters beyond tidiness. The truncation case slices this fixture in half,
# so where the cut falls moves with the line endings; and `#load` embeds
# whatever is on disk, so a rewritten checkout tests a different file while
# every name in the suite stays the same.
#
# -text turns conversion off in both directions: what is committed is what is
# checked out is what the Engine wrote. Written for every package's fixtures
# directory rather than for this one file, because the reason is about fixtures
# and not about JSON.
**/fixtures/** -text
